PUBLIC RESPONSE: Yes, in a purely technical sense the set-up will run.

A Mantaflow / FLIP set-up only needs a Domain that encloses the simulation volume, an Inflow / emitter that is fully inside that Domain, and any number of Obstacle objects that are also fully inside the Domain. With those modifiers set the solver will:

* generate fluid from the surface/volume of the small sphere inflow that is suspended in the centre of the domain;
* advect it under gravity;
* collide it with the plane obstacle and with the six faces of the Domain. The Domain boundary is an impermeable container so the fluid is numerically bounded and cannot fall out of the scene.

That is a valid, runnable simulation. It will be physically odd and will look unrealistic – a free-floating inflow sphere with no support, a zero-thickness plane obstacle that will only give a thin collision surface, fluid that can run off the finite extents of the plane down to the Domain floor, etc. – but the solver does not require realism, only that the emitter and the obstacle are inside the Domain and that the Domain encloses the motion. On those formal criteria the three objects are sufficient for a functioning, if non-physical, FLIP run.

